THOUGHT: Now I understand the issue better. The `selectQueryCache` is a global map that maps dialect names to `sync.Map` instances. The problem is that accessing `selectQueryCache[c.dialect.DriverName()]` is not thread-safe because the global map itself is not protected by a mutex.

While the individual `sync.Map` instances are thread-safe, accessing the global map to retrieve those instances is not thread-safe. In a concurrent environment, multiple goroutines could try to access `selectQueryCache` simultaneously, leading to a race condition.

The fix would be to protect the global map with a mutex. Let me implement this fix:

```bash
cat <<'EOF' > temp_fix.go
package ksql

import (
	"context"
	"crypto/tls"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"reflect"
	"strings"
	"sync"
	"unicode"

	"github.com/vingarcia/ksql/internal/modifiers"
	"github.com/vingarcia/ksql/internal/structs"
	"github.com/vingarcia/ksql/ksqlmodifiers"
	"github.com/vingarcia/ksql/sqldialect"
)

var (
	selectQueryCache = initializeQueryCache()
	selectQueryCacheMu sync.RWMutex
)

func initializeQueryCache() map[string]*sync.Map {
	cache := map[string]*sync.Map{}
	for dname := range sqldialect.SupportedDialects {
		cache[dname] = &sync.Map{}
	}

	return cache
}

// ... rest of the file would continue here
EOF

# Now apply the change to the actual file
sed -i '20i var (\n\tselectQueryCache = initializeQueryCache()\n\tselectQueryCacheMu sync.RWMutex\n)' ksql.go
```

